Chapter: Pr87Alias
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 PR 87 falsifier 1, the shape the Cobblestone lane's seven arms did not
 cover: a self tail call at NON-FULL arity reached through a let-bound
 ALIAS of the definition rather than through its own name.

 PREDICTION, written before this was compiled. Two things, and they
 point in opposite directions.

 The TYPE CHECKER should refuse it exactly as it refused their arm A:
 `g x` has a function type, the body must have `fa`'s return type
 Integer, so CDX2001. The alias changes how the call is spelled, not
 what it means.

 But if it DID compile, `is-self-call` (Emit/X86_64.codex:75-80) would
 not fire, because it walks the apply spine to an IrName and compares
 that name to the definition's. Through an alias the spine root is `g`,
 not `fa`. So the alias defeats the gate in the SAFE direction -- a
 missed tail call, not an unsafe one -- and it is therefore not a route
 to the shape PR 87 worried about.

 A null result here is a reportable result: they asked for it either way.

Section: Functions

  fa : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  fa (x) (y) = let g = fa in g x

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (fa 1 2))
  end
